etastasis is the leading cause of death in patients with lung cancer, and the brain is one of the most common sites for lung cancer metastasis.1 Radiotherapy is one of the most efficient treatment strategies for lung cancer brain metastasis (LCBM).2 Although standard treatment strategies are used, approximately 30% of patients experience local failure after treatment.3 Therefore, accurate pretherapeutic prediction of
the treatment outcome of the patient with LCBM receiving radiotherapy is of great clinical value. Intratumoral heterogeneity, which generally exists in brain metastasis (BM), has been recognized as the key contributor to intracranial progression and radiation resistance. Due to this intratumoral heterogeneity, tumors with variable microenvironments may have discrepant responses to standard treatment strategies across different tumor lesions and even different tumor subregions.4-6 Thus, precise assessment of intratumoral heterogeneity may have promising potential in predicting tumor treatment response and improving therapeutic decision-making. Factors such as the Karnofsky Performance Status, patient age, tumor volume, the presence of extracranial metastases, and the number of BMs are used and combined in the literature to determine prognostic indices that have been proposed for the prediction of survival in patients with BM.7 Several new MRIbased techniques, such as chemical exchange saturation transfer, PWI, and MRS, have shown promise for predicting tumor response or prognosis.8-10 However, they did not reveal the potential role of intratumoral heterogeneity in the treatment response prediction of BM.
