Radiomics features derived from MRI of LCBM have been used to predict the treatment response to radiotherapy by measuring the characteristics of the whole-tumor region.11,12 This method regards the tumor as a whole and neglects regional heterogeneity within the tumor. Furthermore, prior approaches calculate the features per sequence without combining multiple sequences (which reflect variable information) simultaneously or quantifying the spatial relationships among voxels. Consequently, imaging biomarkers that reflect the overall characteristics of the whole-tumor region may be inadequate for fully revealing intratumoral heterogeneity and precisely predicting tumor treatment response. Habitat analysis, an emerging approach, explicitly divides tumors into subregions containing clusters of voxels with similar characteristics, allowing better quantification of tumor heterogeneity. With the assistance of unsupervised algorithms and multiparameter MRI, habitat features can depict intratumoral heterogeneity in a more interpretable and multidimensional way.13-15 Recently, several studies have reported that intratumoral heterogeneity quantified with habitat features is strongly related to treatment resistance in glioblastoma, oropharyngeal cancer, and breast cancer.16-18 However, the potential of identifying LCBM at risk of local failure to improve patient management in LCBM has not been validated. Therefore, the purpose of this study was to develop and validate a predictive model on the basis of MRI-based habitat features that could quantify intratumoral heterogeneity to predict progression-free survival (PFS) in patients with LCBM before radiotherapy.
MATERIALS AND METHODS Patient Data
This study was approved by the institutional review board of the First Affiliated Hospital of Fujian Medical University (MRCTA, ECFAH of FMU [2020] 192), and informed consent was waived due to its retrospective nature. On the basis of the inclusion and exclusion criteria shown in Fig 1A, we retrospectively enrolled patients with LCBM who received radiotherapy between January 2018 and November 2022. When patients underwent radiotherapy for multiple lesions, up to 3 lesions were considered for inclusion, prioritized by
size.14 The patients who received radiotherapy from January 2018 to February 2022 were included as the primary cohort and were randomly assigned to training and random test (R-test) cohorts at a ratio of 7:3 by reference to the number of progression/nonprogression cases. Patients who received radiotherapy from March 2022 to November 2022 were defined as the time-independent test (TI-test) cohort. Baseline clinical characteristics, including age, sex, the histologic subtype of lung cancer, number of metastases, targeted drug treatment, history of smoking, neurologic symptoms, Karnofsky Performance Status score, tumor location, extracranial metastasis, radiotherapy technique, and the biologically effective dose, were collected from our medical records.
Treatment Response Assessment and Follow-Up
The baseline imaging was acquired before the initiation of radiotherapy. Subsequently, MRI follow-up scans were usually performed at intervals of every 2–3 months. Tumor treatment response was assessed on the basis of the Response Assessment in Neuro-Oncology Brain Metastases (RANO-BM) criteria at the lesion level according to previous literature.19 A maximum unidimensional diameter of the tumor exceeding 20% on follow-up postcontrast T1-weighted imaging (T1CE) was defined as progression. The response assessment was conducted by 2 radiologists with 5 and 20 years of experience in BM diagnosis. Disagreements were reviewed by the 2 radiologists, and a consensus was reached by discussion. PFS was selected as the clinical end point in this study, which was defined as the time from the date of receiving radiotherapy to the date of progression or the last imaging examination during follow-up. The cutoff follow-up time point of this study was January 23, 2024.
MRI Data Acquisition and Preprocessing
For each patient, pretreatment T1CE and FLAIR images were obtained on 1.5T or 3T MR scanners, and the details of the acquisition parameters for MRI are shown in the Supplemental Data. All MR images underwent a standard image-preprocessing protocol, including skull-stripping, N4 bias field correction, registration, and intensity normalization. The first step is to perform skull-stripping using FMRIB Software Library, Version 5.0.8,
SUMMARY
PREVIOUS LITERATURE: Several new MRI-based techniques, such as chemical exchange saturation transfer, PWI, and MRS, have shown promise for predicting tumor response or prognosis. Also, radiomics features derived from MRI of LCBM have been used to predict the treatment response to radiotherapy by measuring the characteristics of the whole-tumor region in previous studies. However, they did not reveal the potential role of intratumoral heterogeneity on treatment outcome prediction of brain metastasis.
KEY FINDINGS: A risk model based on habitat feature–quantified intratumoral heterogeneity could be a reliable biomarker to predict PFS in patients with LCBM receiving radiotherapy.
KNOWLEDGE ADVANCEMENT: The intratumoral heterogeneity of LCBM could be quantified via MRI-based habitat features. A habitat risk model enabled satisfactory PFS prediction for patients with LCBM receiving radiotherapy, having considerable potential for supporting decision-making in clinical practice.
