Tumor Segmentation
ROIs of lesions were segmented using 3D Slicer software (Version 5.0.3; http://www.slicer.org). These tumor ROIs were manually annotated layer by layer along the tumor margins by a radiologist (radiologist A) with 5 years of diagnostic experience in BM MRI. The ROIs were delineated according to an enhanced margin of tumor on T1CE images while excluding T2FLAIR perienhancing alterations. Both cystic and necrotic areas of the tumors were included in the ROIs. Afterward, all MRIs and ROIs were resampled to 1 1 1 mm with SimpleITK. Thirty lesions were randomly chosen for repetitive tumor ROI segmentation, performed by 2 radiologists (radiologists A and B, with 5 and 7 years of experience in BM MRI, respectively) to explore the intraobserver and interobserver stability of features.
Subregion Clustering and Feature Extraction
A total of 198 radiomics features were extracted from the wholetumor region, including 17 histogram-based and 75 texture-based features for each sequence and 14 shape-based features of the whole-tumor region. Then we analyzed all voxels of T1CE and FLAIR using the nonsupervised clustering method, K-means, to split the ROIs into 2, 3, 4, and 5 cluster regions by the in-house-developed software nnFAE (Version 0.1.0) based on Python (Version 3.9.10) and scikit-learn (Version 1.6.0; https://github.com/scikit-learn/ scikit-learn). The clustering results were evaluated using the average Calinski–Harabasz score to determine the optimal number of clusters (k1⁄4 5 in this study, Supplemental Data). After subregion clustering, habitat features were extracted from the intratumoral subregions in this study. Initially, 36 features were extracted from each subregion of the tumor regions, including 34 histogram features (17 features for each sequence), the volume of voxels, and the volume proportion of each subregion. To further measure tumor heterogeneity, we constructed a habitat map in which the intensity values of voxels were replaced
with the previously identified habitat index. We adopted the concept of quantitative imaging features from radiomics studies to measure the spatial heterogeneity among the identified subregions.20 Therefore, 44 features were calculated from the habitat map, including 4 histogram features, 24 gray-level co-occurrence matrix features, and 16 gray-level size zone matrix features. The habitat and radiomics features extracted in this study complied with the image biomarker standardization initiative. The details of the habitat and radiomics features extracted in this study are summarized in the Supplemental Data.
Feature Selection and Modeling
Initially, only radiomics and habitat features with both intra- and interobserver intraclass correlation coefficients (ICCs) .0.75 were retained for further analysis. Then, univariable Cox proportional hazard (Cox PH) analysis was conducted to identify potential risk factors from the clinical, radiomics, and habitat features, and factors with P values ,.10 were retained for subsequent survival analysis using FeAture Explorer (FAE, Version 0.6.0).21 Then the features were standardized with the min-max method due to the significant dimension difference. To reduce the dimensions of the row space of the feature matrix, we used the Pearson correlation coefficient for each pair of features. If the Pearson correlation coefficient exceeded 0.99, we randomly removed 1 of the correlated features. Then, these features were input into a clustering procedure to select the most relevant feature subset. To prevent overfitting of the model, we limited the maximum number of the selected features in the subset to no more than 10% of the sample size used for training in this study. The selected feature subsets were subsequently trained using the Cox PH model with 5-fold cross-validation to determine the optimal number of features. Ultimately, the clinical-, radiomics-, and habitat-based machine learning risk models were built to predict the PFS of lesions.
