For hybrid risk model construction, clinical and habitat features with P values under .10 in the univariate Cox PH analysis were integrated. The hybrid model was developed following a pipeline consistent with that used for the other risk models, including feature matrix normalization (min-max method), Pearson correlation coefficient for featured dimension reduction, cluster for feature selection, and Cox PH for model fit with 5-fold cross-validation. Fig 1B shows the workflow of this study.
Prognostic Value of the Risk Models
For each of the 4 risk models, its potential association with PFS was evaluated in the training, R-test, and TI-test cohorts using Kaplan-Meier analysis. The lesions in the training cohort were
stratified into high-risk and low-risk groups based on the optimal cutoff of the prediction probability of models with X-tile software (Version 3.6.1; https://x-tile.software.informer.com/3.6/). The cutoff values were then applied to the R-test and TI-test cohorts. A log-rank test was used to assess the differences in survival between the 2 risk groups.
Statistical Analysis
Statistical analysis was performed using Python (Version 3.11) and R software (Version 4.4.2; http://www.r-project.org/). Categoric variables were compared using the x2 or Fisher exact test. Continuous variables were compared using the Student t test or the Mann–Whitney U test as appropriate. The clinical features among groups were compared with the CBCgrps (Version 2.8.2) package.
