RESULTS
Patient Characteristics
A total of 146 lesions from 68 patients with LCBM were retrospectively identified. No significant difference was observed in clinical characteristics between the training and R-test cohorts (Supplemental Data); no significant difference was found among the 3 cohorts, except for sex (P 1⁄4 .003) and biologically effective dose (BED) (P 1⁄4 .002) (Supplemental Data).
Feature Selection and Development of Prediction Models After the ICC analysis, 192 radiomics features and 187 habitat features were retained for subsequent analysis. Univariable Cox PH analysis identified 4 clinical, 26 radiomics, and 11 habitat
features potentially associated with lesions’ PFS (P,.10), as detailed in the Supplemental Data. After dimensionality reduction and feature selection, the clinical, radiomics, habitat, and hybrid risk models incorporated 2, 7, 5, and 8 features, respectively. The selected features for each risk model are summarized in Table 1.
Performance Evaluation of the Risk Models
The models’ performance is shown in Table 2 and Fig 2. The habitat risk model demonstrated superior discrimination compared with the other risk models in the TI-test cohort (C-Index: 0.716, 95% CI: 0.548–0.890; Brier score: 0.171, 95% CI: 0.086–0.292). The hybrid model outperformed the other models in the R-test cohort (C-Index: 0.748, 95% CI: 0.575–0.910; Brier score: 0.268, 95% CI: 0.082–0.536). Additionally, the habitat and radiomics risk models showed better performance than the clinical risk model in the TI-test cohort (C-Index: 0.630–0.716 versus 0.377).
Prognostic Value of the Risk Models
In the TI-test cohort, only the PFS in the risk groups defined by the habitat risk model showed significant differences (P 1⁄4 .042) (Fig 3). Figure 4 presents the habitat characteristics of tumors in 2 cases.
