PFS in patients with LCBM receiving radiotherapy. The habitat risk model obtained satisfactory results (C-Index: 0.716), outperforming the clinical, radiomics, and hybrid risk models (C-Index: 0.377–0.630) in the TI-test cohort. Quantitative MRI biomarkers extracted from traditional or advanced MRI that characterize the cellular, biophysical, microstructural, and metabolic features of tumors have been proposed as promising markers for the prediction of treatment outcomes in those with BMs.9,22 However, they did not reveal the potential role of intratumoral heterogeneity on BM’s treatment response prediction. Lee et al23 discovered that the structural and physiologic MRI habitat volume was linked to BM recurrence risk and site after stereotactic radiosurgery. Our study also showed similar results and yielded a comparable prediction ability with the previous study (highest C-Index, previous study versus our study, 0.71 versus 0.716). The habitat features extracted in the present study were derived by combining T1CE and FLAIR images. In previous radiomics studies,24,25 the radiomics features derived from T1CE and FLAIR images were calculated separately and combined only at the level of the overall tumor area. In our study, each voxel in the tumor area was characterized by T1CE and FLAIR simultaneously. We combined multiple physiologic properties at the level of a single voxel. Consequently, with this framework, both the physiologic heterogeneity and the spatial heterogeneity of the LCBM could be fully identified. Compared with the prognostic predictive ability of the hybrid risk model, the habitat risk model showed better predictive performance in the TI-test cohort, indicating that the addition of clinical features failed to improve the prediction efficiency of the habitat risk model in predicting PFS in patients with LCBM receiving radiotherapy, which was inconsistent with the findings of previous studies.18,26,27 One explanation could be that the prediction ability of the clinical features is not as stable as that of habitat features making the relatively low C-Index of clinical modes in the TI-test data set, which in turn weakens the predictive ability of the hybrid risk model.
This study has several limitations. First, the study was retrospective and single-center in nature and involved relatively small sample sizes. However, multiple test cohorts and multiple statistical metrics were used to evaluate the model performance, which improved the reliability of our results. Another limitation of our study was the determination of lesion end points. Due to the low resection rate of BM in this cohort upon progression, local failure was determined radiographically based on a measure of tumor size according to the RANO-BM criteria. Histopathologic analysis is the gold standard for determining radiation necrosis versus tumor progression. Third, as a retrospective study, the slice thickness and gap varied among different scanners. Although the standard resample preprocessing was applied, a thinner slice thickness (eg, 1 mm) may improve the accuracy of assessment of tumor characteristics. In the future, a prospective study with thinner slice thickness may be adapted to improve the model performance. Lastly, the use of cluster data may introduce some problems such as intracluster correlation, reduced statistical power, and potential bias due to confounding factors. The approach of our study is consistent with recent landmark studies, which also include multiple lesions from 1 patient as independent samples without additional statistical analyses.11,12,28 Future multicenter studies with larger cohorts will be conducted and a single-lesion-per-patient selection protocol will be employed to maintain sample independence while improving demographic representation.
CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, the intratumoral heterogeneity of LCBM could be quantified via MRI-based habitat features. A habitat risk model enabled satisfactory PFS prediction for patients with LCBM receiving radiotherapy, holding considerable potential for supporting decision-making in clinical practice.
